# Generated 2026-11-06
# Base: seleron/vitabrowser (patch stack through 378)
#
# Patch 192 declined every local block insertion and removal -- and 378's
# no-op -- on a page whose CSS has a structural pseudo-class (:first-child,
# :nth-*, :last-*, :only-*, *-of-type) or a counter anywhere: by the r103
# census, youtube's five no-op answers all met it.
#
# Inserting or removing a child moves the child index of the parent's
# children only. html_rl_block_struct_risk() therefore asks:
#  - per sheet (cached as patch 186 caches its sibling rules: handle,
#    source pointer, size, FNV-1a hash), the compound around each
#    structural pseudo-class -- its tag, id and classes; attributes and
#    other pseudo-classes ignored (a broader match: safe); one that does
#    not parse simply (quotes, escapes, a namespace, one inside a
#    functional pseudo such as :not(:first-child)) is unbounded;
#  - per change, whether one can match a child element of THIS parent --
#    an *-of-type one only when a moved element has its tag. Over 1000
#    children, or 512 compounds in a sheet: risk, as before.
#  - counters apart: 378's no-op ignores them (an element with no box
#    counts nothing); a block insertion or removal declines when a removed
#    box takes part in counting (374's html_rl_box_counts) and hands a new
#    block whose build touched counters to the full reconvert (its failure
#    path now through 374's html_rl_drop_new).
# html_rl_block_list_style_risk and html_rl_css_has_list_style_risk go.
#
# Result on a 2500-paragraph sim page with :nth-child and :first-child
# rules elsewhere: a block inserted into a box 109 -> 7 ms; a <script>
# appended to <body> 107 -> 4 ms.
#
